// The onboarding module's Thai/English dictionary.
//
// Migrated from lib/translations.ts, which mapped an exact English SENTENCE to
// its Thai translation and was consumed as t("Some English sentence"). Two
// things changed and one thing deliberately did not:
//
//   * SHAPE — one entry per key holding { th, en }, which is what
//     shared/src/i18n.jsx merges over commonDictionary. Thai is the default
//     language there, so th is written first in each pair.
//   * KEYS — stable dot keys ('content.requiredDocuments') instead of the
//     English text itself. A string-keyed dictionary breaks the moment anyone
//     fixes a typo in the source copy: the key changes, the lookup misses, and
//     the screen silently falls back to English with no error anywhere.
//   * THAI — carried across BYTE FOR BYTE. Every one of the 481 translations
//     is the exact string from translations.ts; none was re-typed or
//     re-translated. The English side is recovered from the old key, so the
//     pair is exactly what the module already shipped.
//
// The content.* keys below are generated from that migration and keep their
// original order. Module UI copy that never had a Thai translation (the
// admin editor, error messages, the sign-in gate) is written by hand at the
// top, because those strings are new to this port.
//
// tContent() in lib/contentText.js resolves a raw English content string to
// its dot key — the department content in src/data/ is a large body of English
// text that is not worth rewriting into key references, so it is looked up by
// value at render time. New UI copy should use a dot key directly.

